[{"category":"section","location":"#ParameterSpaces","page":"Home","text":"ParameterSpaces.jl describes constrained model parameters and maps a flat unconstrained vector to the parameters in their natural Julia representation. The unconstrained side is deliberately simple for optimizers; the constrained side preserves scalars, vectors, matrices, and coupled tuples.\n\nusing ParameterSpaces\n\np = (Id(:μ), Pos(:σ), Simplex(:weights, 3))\nθ = zeros(dimension(p))\nη = constrain(p, θ)\n# (0.0, 1.0, [1/3, 1/3, 1/3])\n\nAssociate a space with an arbitrary object by extending param_space:\n\nimport ParameterSpaces: param_space\n\nstruct MyModel\n    n::Int\nend\n\nparam_space(m::MyModel) = (Id(:μ), Pos(:σ), Simplex(:weights, m.n))\n\nThe bundled Distributions.jl extension uses exactly this mechanism. The core package itself has no dependency on Distributions.jl.","title":"ParameterSpaces"},{"category":"section","location":"#Space-constructors","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Space constructors"},{"category":"section","location":"#Scalar-spaces","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Scalar spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"#Related-scalar-parameters","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Related scalar parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"#Vector,-matrix,-and-structured-spaces","page":"Home","text":"SPD(:Σ, n) describes an arbitrary symmetric positive-definite matrix, while Correlation(:R, n) additionally fixes the diagonal to one. The latter has n(n-1)/2 unconstrained coordinates and returns a full n × n correlation matrix.","title":"Vector, matrix, and structured spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"#Naming-wrappers","page":"Home","text":"Tuples of spaces form Cartesian products. Vector and matrix spaces remain one logical parameter each: parameter_symbols(RealVec(:μ, 3)) == (:μ,), and parameter_symbols(SPD(:Σ, 3)) == (:Σ,).","title":"Naming wrappers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Object-mapping","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Object mapping"},{"category":"section","location":"#Distributions.jl","page":"Home","text":"When a space is determined by a Distributions.jl type, both type-level and instance-level mappings are available. Runtime shape information still requires an instance for multivariate, matrix-variate, mixture, and similar models.\n\nusing ParameterSpaces\nusing Distributions\n\nparameter_symbols(param_space(Normal))\n(:μ, :σ)\n\nFor structured parameters the constrained result has the constructor-level shape:\n\nX = MvNormal(zeros(3), [1.0 0.2 0.1; 0.2 1.0 0.3; 0.1 0.3 1.0])\np = param_space(X)\nθ = unconstrained_example(p)\nη = constrained_example(p)\n(size(η[1]), size(η[2]), keys(constrained_namedtuple(p, θ)))\n((3,), (3, 3), (:μ, :Σ))\n\nFor the canonical multivariate normal parameterization, the precision matrix J is positive definite but otherwise unconstrained, so it uses SPD(:J, n).","title":"Distributions.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Transformation-interface","page":"Home","text":"The public transformation interface is intentionally small:\n\nη = constrain(p, θ)\nθ = unconstrain(p, η)\n\nθ is always a flat vector. η has the natural constrained representation: a scalar parameter is a scalar, a vector parameter is a vector, a structured matrix parameter is a full matrix, and a Cartesian product is a tuple of logical parameter values.\n\nUse dimension for the optimizer dimension, and parameter_symbols for logical parameter names. constrained_namedtuple(p, θ) combines those logical names with the natural constrained values.","title":"Transformation interface"},{"category":"section","location":"#Automatic-differentiation","page":"Home","text":"There is no differentiation-specific extension. constrain is implemented with ordinary generic Julia operations, so AD packages can differentiate through it directly.\n\nusing ForwardDiff, ParameterSpaces\n\np = (Id(:μ), Pos(:σ))\nθ = [0.3, -0.2]\n\nForwardDiff.gradient(θ) do x\n    μ, σ = constrain(p, x)\n    μ^2 + σ\nend\n\n","title":"Automatic differentiation"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.Id","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter named name with values in ℝ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.Id"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.Pos","page":"Home","text":"A strictly positive scalar parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.Pos"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.NonNeg","page":"Home","text":"A nonnegative scalar parameter; zero maps back to -Inf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.NonNeg"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.Neg","page":"Home","text":"A strictly negative scalar parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.Neg"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.Prob","page":"Home","text":"A probability parameter in [0, 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.Prob"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.ProbOpen","page":"Home","text":"A probability parameter in (0, 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.ProbOpen"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.ProbOpenLeft","page":"Home","text":"A probability parameter in (0, 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.ProbOpenLeft"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.ProbOpenRight","page":"Home","text":"A probability parameter in [0, 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.ProbOpenRight"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.Lower","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter strictly greater than lower.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.Lower"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.LowerClosed","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter greater than or equal to lower.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.LowerClosed"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.Bounded","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter in [lower, upper].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.Bounded"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.BoundedOpen","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter in (lower, upper).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.BoundedOpen"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.BoundedOpenLeft","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter in (lower, upper].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.BoundedOpenLeft"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.BoundedOpenRight","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter in [lower, upper).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.BoundedOpenRight"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.Ordered","page":"Home","text":"Two real scalar parameters satisfying first < second.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.Ordered"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.PosOrdered","page":"Home","text":"Two positive scalar parameters satisfying 0 < first < second.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.PosOrdered"},{"category":"type","location":"#ParameterSpaces.Between","page":"Home","text":"Three scalar parameters satisfying lower ≤ value ≤ upper.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.Between"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.RealVec","page":"Home","text":"An unconstrained real vector parameter with n entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.RealVec"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.PosVec","page":"Home","text":"An n-component vector parameter with strictly positive entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.PosVec"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.ProbVec","page":"Home","text":"An n-component vector parameter with entries in [0, 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.ProbVec"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.RealMat","page":"Home","text":"An unconstrained real m × n matrix parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.RealMat"},{"category":"type","location":"#ParameterSpaces.Simplex","page":"Home","text":"A probability vector of length n constrained to the simplex.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.Simplex"},{"category":"type","location":"#ParameterSpaces.SPD","page":"Home","text":"An n × n symmetric positive-definite matrix parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.SPD"},{"category":"type","location":"#ParameterSpaces.Correlation","page":"Home","text":"An n × n positive-definite correlation-matrix parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.Correlation"},{"category":"type","location":"#ParameterSpaces.Prefixed","page":"Home","text":"Prefix the logical parameter names of space without changing its values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.Prefixed"},{"category":"function","location":"#ParameterSpaces.param_space","page":"Home","text":"param_space(object)\n\nReturn the parameter-space description associated with object.\n\nparam_space is an open generic function. ParameterSpaces.jl provides the space constructors and transformations, while downstream packages define methods for their own objects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParameterSpaces.param_space"}]
